    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In the Balcones Fault Zone, Texas, vertically segmented normal faults developed in lithologically heterogeneous carbonate units through the formation and interaction of two fundamental structural elements: pressure solutions which form preferentially in marl-rich units, and opening mode fractures which form preferentially in dolostone units. We identified different generations of pressure solutions in marl-rich unit by field mapping and laboratory analysis then documented how these structures link together with each other and with fractures in dolostone unit to form segmented normal faults. First generation structures form in response to overburden loading and consist of bed parallel solution seams in marl-rich unit and bed perpendicular, mode I fractures in dolostone unit. Two additional generations of pressure solution seams form as regional subsidence and bed tilting lead to shearing along the bed parallel pressure solutions. The later generations of pressure solution seams are found branching out from the tips of the bed parallel pressure solution seams, indicating that the bed-parallel seams were sheared. They each form at a low angle to the bed-parallel pressure solutions; one dips to the northwest and the other dips to the southeast.\r\n\r\nAll three generations of pressure solution seams contribute to the formation of normal faults. Continued shearing links segments of second and third generation pressure solutions with bed-parallel pressure solutions to form slip surfaces that initiate normal faults. Linking of vertical fractures in dolostone unit and low angle slip planes in marl-rich unit creates the segmented normal faults found in these carbonate formations. Where these normal faults intercept dolostone units an increased fracture density is observed which is related to the magnitude and direction of slip along the faults. The results of our work contain implications about fluid flow within carbonate reservoirs and aquifers. We illustrate these by calculating the principal components of permeability enhanced by localized dilation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
